details.image_prompt:
[
  "Shot with a Canon EOS R5, 85mm f/1.8 lens. The scene features two rustic, wide-rimmed matte ceramic bowls of chicken, mushroom, and wild rice soup, centered in the frame with generous negative space for cropping. The camera angle is a 3/4 overhead view, roughly 40 degrees, allowing a clear look into the textures of both bowls. One bowl is in sharp focus in the immediate foreground, while the second is softly blurred just behind it. Shallow depth of field creates a beautiful, natural bokeh. Soft, natural side light spills from a window on the left, casting gentle, diffused shadows. The surface is a worn, aged oak tabletop with visible wood grain and small, authentic imperfections. Props include a single wooden spoon resting naturally on the table next to the bowls, a slightly rumpled linen napkin tucked partially under the foreground bowl, and a few stray fresh thyme leaves and a small piece of a cremini mushroom scattered near the edge of the wood surface. Inside the bowls, the soup is a comforting, rustic blend: bite-sized, lightly sautéed white chunks of skinless chicken breast are visible amidst soft, earthy brown cremini mushroom slices and vibrant orange shredded carrots. The wild rice grains are dark and distinct, swimming in a clear, savory golden-amber broth, with bright green spinach leaves wilted gently into the mixture. The broth shows a slight, appetizing sheen of olive oil. The scene is photorealistic, not AI-generated looking; it feels like a lived-in, warm home kitchen. Include slight natural imperfections: a tiny splash mark on the rim of the bowl and unevenly distributed ingredients. No plastic sheen, no artificial symmetry, no hands, no faces. The color palette is cozy and warm, reflecting a golden-hour glow. Indistinguishable from professional editorial food photography for a cookbook."
]
